---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import PaperHeader from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/PaperHeader.astro";
import Equation from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Equation.astro";
import M from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/M.astro";
import Cite from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Cite.astro";
import BibTeX from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/BibTeX.astro";
import PubList from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/PubList.astro";
import Figure from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Figure.astro";
import Example from "../../components/Example.astro";
import { u } from "../../data/nav";
import Code from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Code.astro";
import Note from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Note.astro";
import Stability from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Stability.astro";
const rows = `<ul class="ply-wall ply-wall--lead" role="list">
  <li class="ply-wall__row">
    <h3 class="ply-wall__name"><a href="#">Topological deep learning</a></h3>
    <div class="ply-wall__body ply-wall__body--split">
      <p class="ply-wall__about">Neural networks for data in which relations join more than two things at once.</p>
      <ul class="ply-wall__list" role="list">
        <li><a href="#">Example title: higher-order message passing</a> <span class="ply-wall__aside">Example Venue 2026</span></li>
        <li><a href="#">Example title: a benchmark for simplicial learning</a> <span class="ply-wall__aside">arXiv preprint</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </li>
  <li class="ply-wall__row">
    <h3 class="ply-wall__name"><a href="#">Sheaves</a></h3>
    <div class="ply-wall__body ply-wall__body--split">
      <p class="ply-wall__about">Local data glued along restriction maps.</p>
      <ul class="ply-wall__list" role="list">
        <li><a href="#">Example title: sheaf diffusion with learned restriction maps</a> <span class="ply-wall__aside">Example Workshop 2026</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </li>
</ul>`;
const ledger = `<ul class="ply-ledger">
  <li><span class="ply-ledger__text">Example Prize, for the best student paper at Example Venue</span><span class="ply-ledger__when">2026</span></li>
  <li><span class="ply-ledger__text">Reviewer, Example Conference and Example Workshop</span><span class="ply-ledger__when">2024-2026</span></li>
</ul>`;
const chips = `<ul class="ply-chips">
  <li>Python</li><li>PyTorch</li><li>Rust</li><li>LaTeX</li><li>Astro</li>
</ul>`;
const examplePubs = [
  { year: 2026, title: "Example title: Higher-order message passing on cell complexes", authors: ["A. Author", "John Doe", "B. Author"], venue: "Example Venue 2026", links: [{ label: "Paper", href: "#" }, { label: "Code", href: "#" }] },
  { year: 2026, title: "Example title: a second paper in the same year", authors: ["John Doe", "C. Author"], venue: "Example Workshop 2026", links: [{ label: "Paper", href: "#" }] },
  { year: 2025, title: "Example title: an earlier preprint", authors: ["D. Author", "John Doe"], venue: "arXiv preprint", links: [{ label: "arXiv", href: "#" }] },
];
---
<Docs title="Research" status="experimental" lede="The parts every paper page needs: a header, figures, equations, citations, BibTeX, and a publication list.">
  <Note kind="info">Every title, author, and venue on this page is example content.</Note>

  <h2>Paper header</h2>
  <div class="ex__live" style="border: 2px solid var(--ply-color-rule-strong); padding: 1.5rem">
    <PaperHeader
      level={2}
      venue="Example Venue 2026"
      title="Example title: Higher-order message passing on cell complexes"
      authors={[{ name: "A. Author", affiliations: [1], equal: true }, { name: "John Doe", affiliations: [2], equal: true }, { name: "B. Author", affiliations: [1, 2] }]}
      affiliations={["Example University", "Another Institute"]}
      me="John Doe"
      links={[{ label: "Paper", href: "#", icon: "paper" }, { label: "Code", href: "#", icon: "code" }, { label: "BibTeX", href: "#bibtex", icon: "quote" }]}
    />
  </div>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<PaperHeader
  venue="NeurIPS 2026"
  title="..."
  authors={[{ name: "John Doe", affiliations: [1], equal: true }, ...]}
  affiliations={["Example University"]}
  me="John Doe"
  links={[{ label: "Paper", href: "/paper.pdf", icon: "paper" }, { label: "Code", href: repo, icon: "code" }]}
/>`} />
  <p>The title is the page's <code>h1</code>. When the header sits inside another page, as it does above, pass <code>level={2}</code>. Pass <code>me</code> with your name as it reads in <code>authors</code> to set it in bold, as on the publication wall; the other names drop to regular weight. In plain HTML, give your <code>li</code> the class <code>ply-me</code>.</p>
  <p>The title sizes to the header's own width, not the window's, so it sets the same in a column beside an exhibit as across the page. It runs about 22 characters to a line, and longer lines for a long title, so most titles keep to three lines. A title with a subtitle (the part after the first colon) sets the name in wood type and the subtitle under it in the text face. The colon stays in the heading, hidden, so screen readers and copies keep the whole title. Pass <code>split={"{false}"}</code> to keep the whole title in wood type. In plain HTML, write the subtitle as <code>&lt;span class="ply-visually-hidden"&gt;: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ply-title__sub"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;</code> inside the heading, and set <code>--_len</code> (the length of the name) and <code>--_em</code> (the width of its longest word, from <code>emWidth</code>) on it, as the component does.</p>
  <p>Because the header measures its own width, it takes its width from where it sits, never from its text. In a grid column or a block it fills its space; in a box that shrinks to fit its content, such as an inline-block or a <code>fit-content</code> box, give it a width. The first link is the primary button; give another link <code>primary: true</code> to make it the one, such as the PDF when the first link is the venue's page.</p>
  <h3>Long author lists</h3>
  <p>A report with dozens of authors folds: pass <code>fold</code> with the most names to show whole, and a longer list shows the first half of that number, yours, and the last author, with a gap where names are left out and a button for the whole list. The fold is in the page as built, so nothing moves while it loads. Without script, and on paper, the list shows whole. In plain HTML, load <code>js/fold.js</code> (it is part of <code>js/all.js</code>).</p>
  <div class="ex__live" style="border: 2px solid var(--ply-color-rule-strong); padding: 1.5rem">
    <PaperHeader
      level={4}
      title="Example report: a shared benchmark for cell complexes"
      authors={["A. Author", "B. Author", "C. Author", "D. Author", "E. Author", "F. Author", "G. Author", "H. Author", "John Doe", "I. Author", "J. Author", "K. Author", "L. Author", "M. Author"].map((name) => ({ name }))}
      me="John Doe"
      fold={8}
      venue="Example Workshop 2026"
    />
  </div>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<PaperHeader title="..." authors={authors} me="John Doe" fold={12} />`} />

  <h2>Equations</h2>
  <p>KaTeX renders math at build time, so pages ship HTML and MathML and no math script. The Hodge Laplacian on <M tex={String.raw`k`} />-simplices combines a down and an up part:</p>
  <Equation tex={String.raw`L_k = B_k^{\top} B_k + B_{k+1} B_{k+1}^{\top}`} num={1} />
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<p>... on <M tex="k" />-simplices ...</p>
<Equation tex={String.raw\`L_k = B_k^{\\top} B_k + B_{k+1} B_{k+1}^{\\top}\`} num={1} />`} />
  <p><code>Equation</code> and <code>M</code> bring KaTeX's stylesheet, so only pages with math load it. When the TeX contains a backslash, pass it as <code>tex={"{String.raw`...`}"}</code>: Astro 7 reads backslash sequences inside quoted attributes as escape characters, so <code>\binom</code> in quotes arrives as a backspace and <code>inom</code>. The components stop the build with a clear message if that happens.</p>

  <h2>Citations</h2>
  <p>Hover or focus a citation to see the reference without leaving the sentence<Cite n={1} title="Example reference title" authors="E. Author, F. Author" venue="Example Journal" year={2024} />. The number links to the reference list.</p>
  <ol class="ply-footnotes" style="margin-top: var(--ply-space-5)"><li id="ref-1">E. Author, F. Author. Example reference title. Example Journal, 2024.</li></ol>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<Cite n={1} title="..." authors="..." venue="..." year={2024} />`} />
  <p>The card stays open while the pointer is on it and closes with Escape. In plain HTML, load <code>js/cite.js</code> (it is part of <code>js/all.js</code>).</p>

  <h2>Figures</h2>
  <Figure num={2}>
    <div class="ply-panel" style="aspect-ratio: 16/6; display: grid; place-items: center"><span class="ply-muted">Figure content goes here</span></div>
    <Fragment slot="caption">Captions start with what the figure shows, then how to read it.</Fragment>
  </Figure>
  <p>On an <a href={u("/foundations/layout/#articles")}>article</a> page, <code>margin</code> puts a small figure in the margin after the text, beside the paragraph after it. On narrow screens it stays in the text. Every other figure keeps to the text column, even with <code>wide</code>, which runs a figure past the text only on a docs page.</p>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<Figure num={2} margin>
  <img src={plot.src} alt="..." />
  <Fragment slot="caption">What the figure shows, then how to read it.</Fragment>
</Figure>`} />

  <h2 id="bibtex">BibTeX</h2>
  <p>Long fields wrap under their value, so the whole entry reads before it is copied, and a cite key never breaks unless it is wider than the block. Pass <code>wrap={"{false}"}</code> to let lines scroll instead. <code>Code</code> takes the same <code>wrap</code>.</p>
  <BibTeX entry={`@inproceedings{author2026example,
  title     = {Example title: Higher-order message passing on cell complexes},
  author    = {Author, A. and Doe, John and Author, B.},
  booktitle = {Example Venue},
  year      = {2026}
}`} bib={u("/examples/example.bib")} ris={u("/examples/example.ris")} downloadAttrs={{ "data-track": "citation-download", "data-track-label": "{format}" }} />
  <p>Give <code>bib</code> and <code>ris</code> the addresses of the entry as files, and a row of two small buttons, Download .bib and Download .ris, sits under the panel. Each button downloads its file. <code>downloadAttrs</code> adds attributes to both, such as the data attributes an analytics script reads, and <code>{"{format}"}</code> in a value becomes <code>bib</code> or <code>ris</code>. Each button also has <code>data-format</code> set to its format.</p>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<BibTeX entry={bibtex} bib="/papers/key.bib" ris="/papers/key.ris"
  downloadAttrs={{ "data-track": "citation-download", "data-track-label": "{format}" }} />`} />

  <h2>Publication wall</h2>
  <p>Publications grouped by year, the year in wood type, the site owner's name in bold in each author list.</p>
  <PubList pubs={examplePubs} me="John Doe" />
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<PubList pubs={pubs} me="John Doe" />`} />
  <p>The wall is a region named <code>label</code> ("Publications"). When it sits in a section of yours that has its own heading, pass <code>label={"{false}"}</code>, so the page does not name two regions alike. <code>fold</code> folds a long author list here as in the header, as text: the first names, yours, and the last.</p>

  <h2 id="walls">Walls</h2>
  <p>A wall is a list divided by rules, its labels in one column. The year wall of <code>PubList</code> is one; the same classes set other lists. From 64rem every wall's label column is as wide as an article's rail (<code>--ply-layout-toc</code>), so the labels of every wall, list, and home section start at one edge and what they label starts one column in.</p>
  <h3>Rows</h3>
  <p>A <code>.ply-wall__row</code> sets a name in the label column and what it labels beside it, with a hairline between rows. It suits a list of topics with their papers, or of skills with their tools. Put <code>.ply-wall</code> on a <code>ul</code>, <code>ol</code>, or <code>dl</code>. <code>.ply-wall--lead</code> on the wall adds a short tomato lead above each label, a name here and a year on the year wall. From 80rem, <code>.ply-wall__body--split</code> sets a description and a list in two columns, so the row is as tall as its longer side. <code>.ply-wall__aside</code> is a quiet note after a link, such as a venue.</p>
  <Example html={rows} />
  <h3>Ledger</h3>
  <p>A ledger sets awards, service, and talks: the text at the start of each row and the year at the end, in bold tabular figures, with hairlines between. Set <code>--ply-ledger-when</code> to give the years a color of the site's own.</p>
  <Example html={ledger} />
  <h3>Chips</h3>
  <p>Chips are short names, such as skills or tools, in the mono face. Each sits in a sunken box with a hairline, and the row wraps.</p>
  <Example html={chips} />
</Docs>
